course: dictionary
lesson: 10508
title: Twilight: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Twilight is the dim light in the sky just after the sun sets, or just before it rises. Context helps you pick the right meaning. «Dive down and the light fades like evening: below about two hundred metres it is a dim blue twilight» tells you twilight is a kind of dim light, not a time on the clock.
explain: Read the clues around the word. In the sea sentence, «below about a thousand metres sunlight is gone completely» tells you that twilight is the faint light left before the dark. In a story, the same word can tell you how a time feels: mysterious and gloomy, when bats come out.
explain: Twilight can also mean the last, weaker stage of something: «the twilight of his life». The clue is the word life. Like the faint light at the end of a day, this is the quieter, final part of it. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Twilight is a noun and an adjective: you can say «twilight» as a thing or use it before a noun, like a twilight sky.
fact: Twilight means the dim light just after sunset or just before sunrise.
fact: Twilight happens because the sun lights up the sky even when it is below the horizon.
fact: The twilight of something means its last, weaker stage.
fact: Safety tip: wear bright clothes when cycling at twilight, because drivers see less.
exercise: «Below about two hundred metres it is a dim blue twilight.» What is twilight here? | A type of deep-sea fish | Full, bright daylight | A loud noise from the deep sea | *A dim light, not full daylight
exercise: Twilight is the dim light in the sky. When does it happen? | Only in the middle of the night | *Just after the sun sets, or just before it rises | At noon, when the sun is highest | When it snows
exercise: In «the twilight of his life», what does twilight mean? | A famous old castle | *The last, weaker stage | The middle of a long holiday | The first, brightest stage
test: Why should you wear bright clothes when cycling at twilight? | The sun is at its strongest then | *Because drivers see less in the dim light | Bright colours keep you warm | Bright clothes make you cycle faster
test: In a story, what feeling does twilight often give a time of day? | Bright and happy, like midday | Loud and busy, like a football match | *Mysterious and gloomy, when bats come out | Cold and wet, like ice on a pond
exam: Which sentence uses twilight to mean the last, weaker stage of something? | «We walked home in the twilight, past the lamps.» | *«The twilight of the old king's reign was quiet and sad.» | «Twilight came early in winter.» | «The dogs barked at twilight.»
